      <div style="background:rgba(212,168,67,0.08); border:1px solid rgba(212,168,67,0.25); border-radius:12px; padding:25px 30px; margin-top:20px;">
        <h2 style="text-align:center; font-family:'Oswald'; color:var(--gold); font-size:1.3em; margin-bottom:18px;">&#128218; AP Exam Review &mdash; Topics 2.5, 2.6, 2.7</h2>
        <div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; gap:25px;">
          <div>
            <h3 style="color:var(--gold); font-family:'Oswald'; font-size:1em; margin-bottom:8px;">Lewis Diagrams (2.5)</h3>
            <ul style="font-size:0.85em; color:var(--text-primary); line-height:1.8;">
              <li><strong>Steps:</strong> Count valence e&minus; &rarr; place bonds &rarr; complete octets &rarr; check formal charges</li>
              <li><strong>Octet rule:</strong> Most atoms want 8 electrons (H wants 2)</li>
              <li><strong>Exceptions:</strong> Be (4 e&minus;), B (6 e&minus;), expanded octets for Period 3+ (d orbitals)</li>
              <li><strong>Multiple bonds</strong> form when there aren&rsquo;t enough electrons for octets with only single bonds</li>
              <li>Always count total valence electrons: add for anions, subtract for cations</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 style="color:var(--gold); font-family:'Oswald'; font-size:1em; margin-top:15px; margin-bottom:8px;">Resonance &amp; Formal Charge (2.6)</h3>
            <ul style="font-size:0.85em; color:var(--text-primary); line-height:1.8;">
              <li><strong>Resonance:</strong> Multiple valid Lewis structures differing only in electron placement</li>
              <li>The real structure is a <strong>hybrid</strong> &mdash; an average of all resonance forms</li>
              <li><strong>Formal charge</strong> = valence e&minus; &minus; lone pair e&minus; &minus; &frac12;(bonding e&minus;)</li>
              <li>Best Lewis structure: <strong>minimize formal charges</strong>; negative FC on more electronegative atom</li>
              <li>Resonance structures have equal bond lengths and strengths (e.g., CO<sub>3</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 style="color:var(--gold); font-family:'Oswald'; font-size:1em; margin-bottom:8px;">VSEPR &amp; Molecular Geometry (2.7)</h3>
            <ul style="font-size:0.85em; color:var(--text-primary); line-height:1.8;">
              <li><strong>VSEPR:</strong> Electron groups repel &rarr; arrange to <strong>maximize distance</strong></li>
              <li><strong>Electron geometry</strong> counts ALL electron groups (bonding + lone pairs)</li>
              <li><strong>Molecular geometry</strong> describes only the <strong>atom positions</strong></li>
              <li><strong>Lone pairs</strong> compress bond angles (take up more space than bonding pairs)</li>
              <li>Key geometries: linear (180&deg;), trigonal planar (120&deg;), tetrahedral (109.5&deg;), trigonal bipyramidal (90/120&deg;), octahedral (90&deg;)</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 style="color:var(--gold); font-family:'Oswald'; font-size:1em; margin-top:15px; margin-bottom:8px;">Hybridization</h3>
            <ul style="font-size:0.85em; color:var(--text-primary); line-height:1.8;">
              <li>2 e&minus; groups &rarr; <strong>sp</strong> (linear, 180&deg;)</li>
              <li>3 e&minus; groups &rarr; <strong>sp&sup2;</strong> (trigonal planar, 120&deg;)</li>
              <li>4 e&minus; groups &rarr; <strong>sp&sup3;</strong> (tetrahedral, 109.5&deg;)</li>
              <li>5 e&minus; groups &rarr; <strong>sp&sup3;d</strong> (trigonal bipyramidal)</li>
              <li>6 e&minus; groups &rarr; <strong>sp&sup3;d&sup2;</strong> (octahedral)</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 style="color:var(--maroon); font-family:'Oswald'; font-size:1em; margin-top:15px; margin-bottom:8px;">Common AP Exam Pitfalls</h3>
            <ul style="font-size:0.85em; color:var(--text-primary); line-height:1.8;">
              <li>Don&rsquo;t confuse <strong>electron geometry</strong> with <strong>molecular geometry</strong></li>
              <li>Lone pairs reduce bond angles: NH<sub>3</sub> is 107&deg; not 109.5&deg;; H<sub>2</sub>O is 104.5&deg;</li>
              <li>A molecule can have polar bonds but be <strong>nonpolar overall</strong> (if symmetric)</li>
              <li>Hybridization = number of electron groups on central atom, NOT total bonds</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
