      <!-- AP Exam Review: Topics 3.3–3.6 -->
      <div class="ap-review-section" style="margin-top:25px; padding:20px; background:rgba(123,31,46,0.08); border-left:4px solid var(--maroon); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;">
        <h3 style="color:var(--maroon); margin-bottom:15px;">AP Exam Review — Topics 3.3–3.6</h3>
        <div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; gap:18px;">
          <div>
            <h4 style="color:var(--gold);">Ideal Gas Law (PV = nRT)</h4>
            <ul style="font-size:0.88em; line-height:1.7;">
              <li><strong>R = 0.08206 L&middot;atm/(mol&middot;K)</strong> or 8.314 J/(mol&middot;K)</li>
              <li><strong>STP:</strong> 0&deg;C (273.15 K) and 1 atm. Molar volume = 22.4 L/mol</li>
              <li><strong>Combined gas law:</strong> P₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂ (constant n)</li>
              <li><strong>Dalton's Law:</strong> P<sub>total</sub> = P<sub>A</sub> + P<sub>B</sub> + &hellip; (partial pressures)</li>
              <li><strong>Mole fraction:</strong> &chi;<sub>A</sub> = n<sub>A</sub>/n<sub>total</sub> = P<sub>A</sub>/P<sub>total</sub></li>
            </ul>
            <h4 style="color:var(--gold); margin-top:12px;">Kinetic Molecular Theory</h4>
            <ul style="font-size:0.88em; line-height:1.7;">
              <li>Gas particles have negligible volume compared to container</li>
              <li>No IMF attractions between particles</li>
              <li>Elastic collisions (KE conserved)</li>
              <li><strong>KE<sub>avg</sub> = (3/2)RT</strong> — depends only on temperature</li>
              <li><strong>u<sub>rms</sub> = &radic;(3RT/M)</strong> — lighter gases move faster at same T</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h4 style="color:var(--gold);">Real Gas Deviations</h4>
            <ul style="font-size:0.88em; line-height:1.7;">
              <li><strong>Van der Waals equation:</strong> (P + an&sup2;/V&sup2;)(V - nb) = nRT</li>
              <li><strong>a correction:</strong> IMF attractions reduce measured P (real P &lt; ideal P)</li>
              <li><strong>b correction:</strong> Finite molecular volume reduces available V (real V &gt; ideal V)</li>
              <li><strong>Most ideal conditions:</strong> High T (KE overcomes IMF) + Low P (molecules far apart)</li>
              <li><strong>Greatest deviation:</strong> Low T + High P (molecules slow, close together)</li>
            </ul>
            <h4 style="color:var(--gold); margin-top:12px;">Common AP Exam Pitfalls</h4>
            <ul style="font-size:0.88em; line-height:1.7; color:#ff9;">
              <li>Temperature MUST be in Kelvin for all gas law calculations</li>
              <li>Average KE depends on T only — NOT on molecular identity</li>
              <li>u<sub>rms</sub> depends on BOTH T and molar mass M</li>
              <li>Maxwell-Boltzmann: higher T = broader, flatter curve shifted right</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

