      <div class="ap-review-section" style="margin-top:25px; padding:20px; background:rgba(123,31,46,0.08); border-left:4px solid var(--maroon); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;">
        <h3 style="color:var(--maroon); margin-bottom:15px;">AP Exam Review &mdash; Topics 4.8&ndash;4.9</h3>
        <div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; gap:18px;">

          <!-- Left Column: Acid-Base (4.8) -->
          <div>
            <h4 style="color:var(--gold);">Acid-Base Reactions (4.8)</h4>
            <ul style="font-size:0.88em; line-height:1.7;">
              <li><strong>Arrhenius model:</strong> Acids produce H&#x207A; in water; bases produce OH&#x207B; in water</li>
              <li><strong>Br&oslash;nsted-Lowry model:</strong> Acids donate protons (H&#x207A;); bases accept protons &mdash; broader than Arrhenius</li>
              <li><strong>Conjugate pairs:</strong> Every acid has a conjugate base (and vice versa) formed by gaining/losing one H&#x207A;</li>
              <li><strong>Strong acids:</strong> HCl, HBr, HI, HNO&#x2083;, H&#x2082;SO&#x2084;, HClO&#x2083;, HClO&#x2084; &mdash; ionize 100% in water</li>
              <li><strong>Strong bases:</strong> Group 1 &amp; 2 hydroxides (NaOH, KOH, Ca(OH)&#x2082;, etc.)</li>
              <li><strong>Weak acids/bases:</strong> Partially ionize; establish an equilibrium (indicated by &#x21CC;)</li>
              <li><strong>Amphiprotic species:</strong> Can act as acid or base (e.g., H&#x2082;O, HCO&#x2083;&#x207B;, HSO&#x2084;&#x207B;)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <!-- Right Column: Redox (4.9) -->
          <div>
            <h4 style="color:var(--gold);">Oxidation States &amp; Redox (4.9)</h4>
            <ul style="font-size:0.88em; line-height:1.7;">
              <li><strong>Free elements:</strong> Oxidation state = 0 (e.g., Na, O&#x2082;, Fe)</li>
              <li><strong>Monatomic ions:</strong> Oxidation state = charge (e.g., Na&#x207A; = +1, Cl&#x207B; = &minus;1)</li>
              <li><strong>Oxygen:</strong> Usually &minus;2 (except in peroxides: &minus;1)</li>
              <li><strong>Hydrogen:</strong> Usually +1 (except in metal hydrides: &minus;1)</li>
              <li><strong>Fluorine:</strong> Always &minus;1</li>
              <li><strong>Sum rule:</strong> Oxidation states in a compound sum to 0; in a polyatomic ion, sum to the ion charge</li>
              <li><strong>Oxidation:</strong> Loss of electrons (state increases) &mdash; &ldquo;OIL&rdquo;</li>
              <li><strong>Reduction:</strong> Gain of electrons (state decreases) &mdash; &ldquo;RIG&rdquo;</li>
              <li><strong>Oxidizing agent:</strong> Gets reduced (gains e&#x207B;); causes oxidation in another species</li>
              <li><strong>Reducing agent:</strong> Gets oxidized (loses e&#x207B;); causes reduction in another species</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common AP Exam Pitfalls — Both Topics -->
        <div style="margin-top:15px; padding:12px 15px; background:rgba(0,0,0,0.15); border-radius:8px; border:1px solid rgba(255,255,153,0.15);">
          <h4 style="color:#ff9; margin-bottom:8px;">Common AP Exam Pitfalls</h4>
          <div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; gap:12px;">
            <ul style="font-size:0.85em; line-height:1.7; color:#ff9;">
              <li>A strong acid is NOT the same as a concentrated acid &mdash; &ldquo;strong&rdquo; means 100% ionization</li>
              <li>Water can be an acid OR a base depending on the reaction (amphiprotic)</li>
              <li>Conjugate base of a strong acid is negligibly weak (e.g., Cl&#x207B; from HCl)</li>
              <li>Neutralization reactions are NOT always redox &mdash; no change in oxidation states</li>
            </ul>
            <ul style="font-size:0.85em; line-height:1.7; color:#ff9;">
              <li>The oxidizing agent is REDUCED (it gains electrons) &mdash; don&rsquo;t confuse the names!</li>
              <li>Combustion IS a redox reaction: carbon is oxidized, oxygen is reduced</li>
              <li>In MnO&#x2084;&#x207B;: Mn = +7 (not +4). Use the sum rule: Mn + 4(&minus;2) = &minus;1</li>
              <li>Activity series: metals above H&#x2082; can displace H&#x207A; from acids</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
