      <div class="learning-goal"><strong>AP Connection:</strong> Write rate law expressions and determine reaction orders from experimental data using the method of initial rates (Topics 5.1–5.2). Calculate rate constants and predict reaction rates for different concentrations.</div>

      <details class="how-to-use"><summary>How to Use This Tool</summary><div class="how-to-content">
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Set the reaction orders</strong> using the sliders for "Order with respect to A" and "Order with respect to B" (0, 1, 2, or 3).</li>
          <li><strong>Adjust the rate constant k</strong> to see how it affects the overall rate.</li>
          <li><strong>Examine the rate law expression</strong> that forms from your chosen orders: Rate = k[A]^m[B]^n.</li>
          <li><strong>Study the data table</strong> showing initial rates for different concentration combinations — this is exactly what you see on the AP exam.</li>
          <li><strong>Practice the method of initial rates:</strong> Look at how doubling [A] affects the rate to determine the order with respect to A.</li>
        </ol>
        <p><strong>Try this:</strong> Set order A = 2 and order B = 1 (overall order = 3). Double [A] — the rate quadruples (2² = 4). Double [B] — the rate only doubles (2¹ = 2). This is how you determine reaction orders experimentally.</p>
        <p><strong>AP Tip:</strong> Rate laws can ONLY be determined experimentally, never from the balanced equation. Compare trials where only ONE concentration changes to find each order.</p>
      </div></details>

      <div class="tool-body" style="display:flex; gap:20px; flex-wrap:wrap;">
        <div class="control-panel" style="flex:1; min-width:300px;">
          <div class="control-group">
            <label>Reaction: A + B → Products</label>
          </div>
