            <h4 style="color:var(--gold);">Integrated Rate Laws</h4>
            <ul style="font-size:0.88em; line-height:1.7;">
              <li><strong>Zero order:</strong> [A] = &minus;kt + [A]₀. Plot [A] vs t &rarr; straight line</li>
              <li><strong>First order:</strong> ln[A] = &minus;kt + ln[A]₀. Plot ln[A] vs t &rarr; straight line</li>
              <li><strong>Second order:</strong> 1/[A] = kt + 1/[A]₀. Plot 1/[A] vs t &rarr; straight line</li>
              <li><strong>Determine order:</strong> Whichever plot gives a straight line reveals the order</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h4 style="color:var(--gold);">Half-Life</h4>
            <ul style="font-size:0.88em; line-height:1.7;">
              <li><strong>Zero order:</strong> t&frac12; = [A]₀ / 2k (depends on [A]₀)</li>
              <li><strong>First order:</strong> t&frac12; = 0.693 / k (constant! Independent of [A]₀)</li>
              <li><strong>Second order:</strong> t&frac12; = 1 / (k[A]₀) (depends on [A]₀)</li>
            </ul>
            <h4 style="color:var(--gold); margin-top:12px;">Common AP Exam Pitfalls</h4>
            <ul style="font-size:0.88em; line-height:1.7; color:#ff9;">
              <li>Only first-order reactions have a constant half-life</li>
              <li>The slope of the linear plot gives k (or &minus;k for zero/first order)</li>
              <li>Radioactive decay is always first order</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
