          <div>
            <h4 style="color:var(--gold);">Collision Theory & Catalysts</h4>
            <ul style="font-size:0.88em; line-height:1.7;">
              <li><strong>Effective collision:</strong> Sufficient energy AND proper orientation</li>
              <li><strong>Arrhenius equation:</strong> k = Ae<sup>&minus;E<sub>a</sub>/RT</sup> (higher T or lower E<sub>a</sub> &rarr; larger k)</li>
              <li><strong>Catalyst:</strong> Lowers E<sub>a</sub> by providing an alternative pathway. Does NOT change &Delta;H</li>
              <li><strong>Catalyst is not consumed:</strong> Appears in mechanism but cancels out overall</li>
            </ul>
            <h4 style="color:var(--gold); margin-top:12px;">Common AP Exam Pitfalls</h4>
            <ul style="font-size:0.88em; line-height:1.7; color:#ff9;">
              <li>A catalyst lowers E<sub>a</sub> for BOTH forward and reverse reactions equally</li>
              <li>A catalyst does NOT shift equilibrium — it speeds up both directions equally</li>
              <li>Multistep: the slow step has the highest E<sub>a</sub> (rate-determining step)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
