          <div>
            <h4 style="color:var(--gold);">Reaction Mechanisms</h4>
            <ul style="font-size:0.88em; line-height:1.7;">
              <li><strong>Elementary step:</strong> A single molecular event. Rate law CAN be written from coefficients</li>
              <li><strong>Overall reaction:</strong> Sum of all elementary steps (intermediates cancel)</li>
              <li><strong>Intermediate:</strong> Produced in one step, consumed in another. Never in overall equation</li>
              <li><strong>Rate-determining step (RDS):</strong> Slowest step — determines overall rate law</li>
              <li><strong>Molecularity:</strong> Unimolecular (1 reactant), Bimolecular (2), Termolecular (rare, 3)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h4 style="color:var(--gold);">Deriving Rate Laws from Mechanisms</h4>
            <ul style="font-size:0.88em; line-height:1.7;">
              <li><strong>Simple case:</strong> If RDS is first step, use its molecularity for rate law</li>
              <li><strong>Pre-equilibrium:</strong> If RDS follows a fast equilibrium, substitute for intermediate using K<sub>eq</sub></li>
              <li><strong>Steady-state:</strong> Rate of formation of intermediate = rate of consumption</li>
              <li><strong>Valid mechanism must:</strong> (1) Sum to overall equation, (2) Agree with experimental rate law</li>
            </ul>
            <h4 style="color:var(--gold); margin-top:12px;">Common AP Exam Pitfalls</h4>
            <ul style="font-size:0.88em; line-height:1.7; color:#ff9;">
              <li>Intermediates appear in mechanisms but NOT in the overall rate law (must be substituted out)</li>
              <li>Catalysts appear in early steps and are regenerated in later steps</li>
              <li>A proposed mechanism must match the experimental rate law to be valid</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
