          <div>
            <h4 style="color:var(--gold);">Hess's Law</h4>
            <ul style="font-size:0.88em; line-height:1.7;">
              <li><strong>Enthalpy is a state function:</strong> &Delta;H depends only on initial and final states, NOT the path</li>
              <li><strong>Hess's Law:</strong> &Delta;H for a reaction = sum of &Delta;H for individual steps</li>
              <li>If a reaction is reversed, the sign of &Delta;H changes</li>
              <li>If a reaction is multiplied by n, &Delta;H is multiplied by n</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h4 style="color:var(--gold);">Using Hess's Law</h4>
            <ul style="font-size:0.88em; line-height:1.7;">
              <li><strong>Strategy:</strong> Manipulate given reactions (reverse, multiply) so they sum to the target</li>
              <li><strong>Cancel intermediates:</strong> Species that appear on both sides cancel out</li>
              <li>Formation enthalpies method is a special case of Hess's Law</li>
            </ul>
            <h4 style="color:var(--gold); margin-top:12px;">Common AP Exam Pitfalls</h4>
            <ul style="font-size:0.88em; line-height:1.7; color:#ff9;">
              <li>When you reverse a reaction, flip the sign: &Delta;H &rarr; &minus;&Delta;H</li>
              <li>When you multiply by 2, double &Delta;H (don't forget!)</li>
              <li>State function means you can take ANY path — the answer is the same</li>
            </ul>
