        <div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; gap:18px;">
          <div>
            <h4 style="color:var(--gold); margin:0 0 6px;">Solubility Product (Ksp)</h4>
            <ul style="margin:0; padding-left:18px; font-size:0.88em; line-height:1.7;">
              <li><b>Ksp =</b> product of ion concentrations raised to stoichiometric powers</li>
              <li><b>Molar solubility (s):</b> Moles of salt that dissolve per liter</li>
              <li>For AB: Ksp = s²; For AB₂: Ksp = 4s³; For A₂B₃: Ksp = 108s⁵</li>
              <li><b>Q vs Ksp:</b> Q > Ksp → precipitate forms; Q < Ksp → no precipitate</li>
              <li><b>Smaller Ksp ≠ always less soluble</b> — must compare molar solubilities</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h4 style="color:var(--gold); margin:0 0 6px;">Common-Ion Effect</h4>
            <ul style="margin:0; padding-left:18px; font-size:0.88em; line-height:1.7;">
              <li><b>Adding a common ion</b> decreases solubility (Le Chatelier shift left)</li>
              <li>Example: Adding NaCl to saturated AgCl reduces Ag⁺ concentration</li>
              <li><b>pH and solubility:</b> Salts of weak acids dissolve more in acidic solutions</li>
              <li><b>Complex ion formation</b> can increase solubility (e.g., AgCl + NH₃)</li>
            </ul>
            <h4 style="color:#e8c36a; margin:10px 0 4px;">⚠️ Common AP Exam Pitfalls</h4>
            <ul style="margin:0; padding-left:18px; font-size:0.85em; line-height:1.7;">
              <li>Don't forget stoichiometric coefficients: PbI₂ gives [Pb²⁺] = s but [I⁻] = 2s</li>
              <li>You can't directly compare Ksp values for salts with different formulas — calculate molar solubility instead</li>
              <li>Ksp applies only to slightly soluble salts, NOT to soluble salts like NaCl</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
