          <li><strong>Study the graph</strong> showing the relationship between ΔG° and ln K at the selected temperature.</li>
        </ol>
        <p><strong>Try this:</strong> Set ΔG° = 0 — K equals exactly 1 (equal amounts of reactants and products at equilibrium). Now make ΔG° = −10 kJ/mol — K jumps to about 57 at 298 K. Make ΔG° very negative (−40 kJ/mol) — K becomes enormous (products strongly favored).</p>
        <p><strong>AP Tip:</strong> ΔG° tells you about the equilibrium position (K). ΔG (without the °) tells you about the direction of reaction under NON-standard conditions: ΔG = ΔG° + RT ln Q. When Q = K, ΔG = 0 (equilibrium).</p>
      </div></details>

      <div class="tool-body" style="display:flex; gap:20px; flex-wrap:wrap;">
        <div class="control-panel" style="flex:1; min-width:280px;">
