      <div style="margin:24px 0 18px; padding:18px 20px; border-left:4px solid var(--maroon); background:rgba(123,31,46,0.08); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;">
        <h3 style="color:var(--gold); margin:0 0 12px;">AP Exam Review &mdash; Topics 9.8&ndash;9.11</h3>
        <div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; gap:18px;">
          <div>
            <h4 style="color:var(--gold); margin:0 0 6px;">Galvanic &amp; Electrolytic Cells</h4>
            <ul style="margin:0; padding-left:18px; font-size:0.88em; line-height:1.7;">
              <li><b>Galvanic (voltaic):</b> Spontaneous reaction &rarr; produces electricity (&Delta;G &lt; 0, E&deg; &gt; 0)</li>
              <li><b>Electrolytic:</b> Non-spontaneous, driven by external power (&Delta;G &gt; 0, E&deg; &lt; 0)</li>
              <li><b>Anode:</b> Oxidation occurs (AN OX); <b>Cathode:</b> Reduction occurs (RED CAT)</li>
              <li><b>E&deg;cell = E&deg;cathode &minus; E&deg;anode</b> (using standard reduction potentials)</li>
              <li><b>Salt bridge:</b> Maintains electrical neutrality; cations migrate toward cathode</li>
              <li><b>As cell runs:</b> anode dissolves (loses mass), cathode gains mass (metal deposits)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h4 style="color:var(--gold); margin:0 0 6px;">Nernst Equation &amp; Faraday&rsquo;s Law</h4>
            <ul style="margin:0; padding-left:18px; font-size:0.88em; line-height:1.7;">
              <li><b>Nernst equation:</b> E = E&deg; &minus; (0.0592/n)log Q &nbsp;(at 25&deg;C)</li>
              <li><b>As Q increases &rarr; E decreases</b> toward zero (equilibrium)</li>
              <li><b>&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;nFE&deg;</b> &nbsp;(F = 96,485 C/mol e&#8315;)</li>
              <li><b>Faraday&rsquo;s Law:</b> mol deposited = (I &times; t) / (n &times; F)</li>
              <li><b>Mass deposited</b> = mol &times; molar mass</li>
            </ul>
            <h4 style="color:#e8c36a; margin:10px 0 4px;">&#9888;&#65039; Common AP Exam Pitfalls</h4>
            <ul style="margin:0; padding-left:18px; font-size:0.85em; line-height:1.7;">
              <li>Never multiply E&deg; by stoichiometric coefficients &mdash; it&rsquo;s an intensive property!</li>
              <li>Galvanic cell: anode is (&minus;), cathode is (+)</li>
              <li>Electrons flow through the wire; <em>ions</em> flow through the salt bridge</li>
              <li>At equilibrium: E = 0, Q = K &mdash; the cell is &ldquo;dead&rdquo;</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Worked Examples 9C -->
      <div style="margin-bottom:18px; padding:15px; background:rgba(205,163,73,0.08); border-left:4px solid var(--gold); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;">
        <h3 style="color:var(--gold); margin-bottom:12px; font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif; font-size:0.95em; letter-spacing:1px; text-transform:uppercase;">Worked Examples &mdash; Electrochemistry</h3>
        <details style="margin-bottom:10px; background:rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border-radius:8px; border:1px solid rgba(205,163,73,0.15); overflow:hidden;">
          <summary style="padding:12px 15px; cursor:pointer; color:var(--gold); font-weight:600; font-size:0.9em; list-style:none;">&#9654; Example 1: Calculate E&deg;cell and &Delta;G&deg; for the Zn/Cu galvanic cell</summary>
          <div style="padding:12px 15px; border-top:1px solid rgba(205,163,73,0.1); font-size:0.85em; line-height:1.8;">
            <p><strong>Cell:</strong> Zn(s) | Zn&#178;&#8314;(aq) || Cu&#178;&#8314;(aq) | Cu(s)</p>
            <p><strong style="color:var(--gold);">Step 1:</strong> Identify half-reactions and standard reduction potentials.<br>
            Cathode (reduction): Cu&#178;&#8314; + 2e&#8315; &rarr; Cu &nbsp; E&deg; = +0.34 V<br>
            Anode (oxidation): Zn &rarr; Zn&#178;&#8314; + 2e&#8315; &nbsp; E&deg;<sub>ox</sub> = +0.76 V</p>
            <p><strong style="color:var(--gold);">Step 2:</strong> E&deg;cell = E&deg;cathode &minus; E&deg;anode (using reduction potentials)<br>
            E&deg;cell = 0.34 &minus; (&minus;0.76) = <strong style="color:var(--gold);">+1.10 V</strong> &nbsp; (positive = spontaneous!)</p>
            <p><strong style="color:var(--gold);">Step 3:</strong> &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;nFE&deg; = &minus;(2)(96485)(1.10) / 1000 = <strong style="color:var(--gold);">&minus;212 kJ/mol</strong></p>
            <div style="padding:8px; background:rgba(123,31,46,0.15); border-radius:4px; margin-top:6px;">
              <strong>Key:</strong> AN OX, RED CAT. Anode always has oxidation. Never flip the sign of E&deg; when calculating E&deg;cell &mdash; just subtract E&deg;anode from E&deg;cathode.
            </div>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details style="margin-bottom:10px; background:rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border-radius:8px; border:1px solid rgba(205,163,73,0.15); overflow:hidden;">
          <summary style="padding:12px 15px; cursor:pointer; color:var(--gold); font-weight:600; font-size:0.9em; list-style:none;">&#9654; Example 2: Apply the Nernst equation at non-standard concentrations</summary>
          <div style="padding:12px 15px; border-top:1px solid rgba(205,163,73,0.1); font-size:0.85em; line-height:1.8;">
            <p><strong>Given:</strong> Zn/Cu cell, [Zn&#178;&#8314;] = 0.10 M, [Cu&#178;&#8314;] = 2.0 M, E&deg;cell = 1.10 V, n = 2</p>
            <p><strong style="color:var(--gold);">Step 1:</strong> Write the reaction quotient Q.<br>
            Q = [Zn&#178;&#8314;] / [Cu&#178;&#8314;] = 0.10 / 2.0 = 0.050</p>
            <p><strong style="color:var(--gold);">Step 2:</strong> Apply Nernst equation at 25&deg;C.<br>
            E = E&deg; &minus; (0.0592/n) log Q<br>
            E = 1.10 &minus; (0.0592/2) log(0.050)<br>
            E = 1.10 &minus; (0.0296)(&minus;1.301) = 1.10 + 0.039 = <strong style="color:var(--gold);">1.139 V</strong></p>
            <div style="padding:8px; background:rgba(123,31,46,0.15); border-radius:4px; margin-top:6px;">
              <strong>Key:</strong> When Q &lt; 1 (products scarce), E &gt; E&deg;. When Q &gt; 1 (products abundant), E &lt; E&deg;. As Q increases toward K, voltage drops to zero.
            </div>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

